      "type": "FIB_RW",
      "frequency": "极高频",
      "passage_html": "<p>Mischel is the creator of the marshmallow test, one of the most famous experiments in the history of psychology, which is often cited as evidence of the importance of self-control. In the original test, which was administered at the Bing Nursery School, at Stanford, in the nineteen-sixties, Mischel's team would present a child with a treat (marshmallows were just one option) and tell her that she could either eat the one treat <select class=\"blank-select\" data-blank-index=\"1\"><option value=\"\">选择答案</option><option value=\"ironically\">ironically</option><option value=\"impressively\">impressively</option><option value=\"immediately\">immediately</option><option value=\"imaginatively\">imaginatively</option></select> or wait alone in the room for several minutes until the researcher returned, at which point she could have two treats. The promised treats were always visible and the child knew that all she had to do to stop the agonizing <select class=\"blank-select\" data-blank-index=\"2\"><option value=\"\">选择答案</option><option value=\"sleep\">sleep</option><option value=\"wait\">wait</option><option value=\"walk\">walk</option><option value=\"time\">time</option></select> was ring a bell to call the experimenter back-- although in that <select class=\"blank-select\" data-blank-index=\"3\"><option value=\"\">选择答案</option><option value=\"quantity\">quantity</option><option value=\"case\">case</option><option value=\"span\">span</option><option value=\"consumption\">consumption</option></select> , she wouldn't get the second treat. The longer a child delayed gratification, Mischel found--that is, the longer she was able to wait-- the better she would fare later in life at numerous measures of what we now call executive function. She would <select class=\"blank-select\" data-blank-index=\"4\"><option value=\"\">选择答案</option><option value=\"slump\">slump</option><option value=\"heave\">heave</option><option value=\"slumber\">slumber</option><option value=\"perform\">perform</option></select> better academically, earn more money, and be healthier and happier. She would also be more likely to avoid a number of negative outcomes, including jail time, obesity, and drug use.</p>",
      "analysis_html": "<p>1、 immediately</p>\n<p>考点：并列结构</p>\n<p>解析：副词 immediately 与后方的 wait 借由并列连词 or 紧密连接,在句中构成了非此即彼的对立和并列。整个句子的字面逻辑在阐述实验的规则:孩子们要么【立即】吃掉面前的那一份奖励,要么在房间里独自等待。由此锁定符合逻辑对仗的 immediately。</p>\n<p>2、 wait</p>\n<p>考点：上下文语境</p>\n<p>解析：在单句语境中,名词充当动词 stop 的宾语。紧接着看前文第二句中反复出现的核心线索——“等待几分钟(wait alone in the room for several minutes)”以及后文第四句的“延迟满足/等待(delayed gratification / able to wait)”,这说明孩子们正在经历的过程就是等待。因此优先锁定名词 wait,精准表达“结束这一令人痛苦的【等待】”。</p>\n<p>3、 case</p>\n<p>考点：固定搭配</p>\n<p>解析：名词 case 与前方的指示代词 that 以及介词 in 构成了极其常用的习惯固定搭配 in that case(在那种情况下)。它在单句中规范地表达了“尽管在【那种情况下】,她将无法得到第二份奖励”。</p>\n<p>4、 perform</p>\n<p>考点：固定搭配</p>\n<p>解析：动词 perform 与后方的副词 better 结合,并与后方的学术领域名词 academically 构成了高频固定搭配 perform better academically(在学业上表现得更好 / 取得更好的学业成绩)</p>",
      "translation_html": "<p>Mischel is the creator of the marshmallow test, one of the most famous experiments in the history of psychology, which is often cited as evidence of the importance of self-control.</p>\n<p>Mischel是棉花糖实验的创始人,该实验是心理学史上最著名的实验之一,经常 被引用为证明自制力重要性的证据。</p>\n<p>In the original test, which was administered at the Bing Nursery School, at Stanford, in the nineteen-sixties, Mischel's team would present a child with a treat (marshmallows were just one option) and tell her that she could either eat the one treat immediately or wait alone in the room for several minutes until the researcher returned, at which point she could have two treats.</p>\n<p>在20世纪60年代,斯坦福大学的宾幼儿园是施行这项原始测试的地方,Mischel 的团队会给孩子一份奖品(棉花糖只是其中一种选项),并告诉她,她可以直接吃掉这份奖品,或者独自在房间里等几分钟,直到研究者回来,这时她可以得到两份奖品。</p>\n<p>The promised treats were always visible and the child knew that all she had to do to stop the agonizing wait was ring a bell to call the experimenter back-- although in that case , she wouldn't get the second treat.</p>\n<p>承诺的奖品始终可⻅,孩子也知道,她只需按铃就能叫回实验者,从而结束令人 痛苦的等待,然而在这种情况下,她得不到第二份奖品。</p>\n<p>The longer a child delayed gratification, Mischel found--that is, the longer she was able to wait-- the better she would fare later in life at numerous measures of what we now call executive function.</p>\n<p>Mischel发现,孩子推迟满足的时间越⻓,即等待时间越⻓,她在后来被我们称 为执行功能的诸多测试中表现就越好。</p>\n<p>She would perform better academically, earn more money, and be healthier and happier.</p>\n<p>她的学业表现会更好,赚更多的钱,而且会更健康、更快乐。</p>\n<p>She would also be more likely to avoid a number of negative outcomes, including jail time, obesity, and drug use.</p>\n<p>她也更有可能避免包括蹲监狱、肥胖和吸毒在内的多种不良结局。</p>",
